WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:25.820
William Wallace: Scotland's larger-than-life
hero

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.620
Thanks to the 1995 Mel Gibson movie Braveheart,
most people are fairly familiar with the name

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.620
of William Wallace.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.690
Unfortunately, thanks to the same film, most
people also have a mental picture of him that

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.079
varies between mildly inaccurate and completely
wrong.

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:43.109
He was, indeed, one of Scotland's most iconic
freedom fighters… wasn't he?

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:47.690
The truth is fairly difficult to track down,
in large part due to the fact that he lived

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.479
in the 13th century.

00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:54.299
Records were iffy at best, and doing even
just a little bit of digging uncovers the

00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:59.449
fact that some of the original sources for
many of the well-known "facts" about Wallace's

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:02.989
life were viewed as dodgy right from the beginning.

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:07.970
Wallace exists in that strange realm somewhere
between historical fact and oft-told legend,

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.490
which means separating fact from fiction can
be tricky at best.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.619
We're going to give it a try.

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:22.330
The misstep of a horse changed the course
of history

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.930
In order to truly understand Wallace — and
why he became such a figurehead for Scotland's

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.250
national identity — it's important to know
a little bit about the world he lived in.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.140
And this starts with Alexander III.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.450
Alexander III ruled Scotland from 1249 to
1286, after being crowned king at just eight

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.450
years old.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.649
That just sounds like a recipe for disaster,
and it nearly was.

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:46.630
While he was still a child, rival factions
within the country struggled for control of

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.289
the young king, but when he turned 21, he
made it clear that the crown was very much

00:01:51.289 --> 00:01:53.909
his own to do with what he pleased.

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:57.960
And one of the first things he did was to
finish what his father had started.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.060
The islands off the coast of his country were
under Norwegian control, and within just a

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:07.120
few years he had done what his father could
not: he got those islands back into Scottish

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.120
control.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:12.010
Meanwhile, at home, he ushered in something
of a golden age for his nation.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.530
Trade flourished, and Scotland was on good
terms not only with the countries and rulers

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.100
of the continent, but of England, too.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.370
The land dedicated to agriculture continued
to sprawl, monasteries and abbeys popped up

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.250
across the country, and these centers of religious
learning flourished.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.710
Life was, for the most part, surprisingly
peaceful.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.060
But, like most good things, it wasn't going
to last.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.800
Alexander first married Margaret and tragically,
but she and their two sons were dead by 1283.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.990
They had a daughter, too, who married Norway's
King Eirik II and had a daughter of her own.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.490
That child — Margaret, the Maid of Norway
— was acknowledged as the heir to the throne,

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.970
but things often don't go as planned.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.160
The king still wanted a male heir, and ended
up remarrying.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.801
He and Yolande de Dreux, Comtesse de Montfort,
tied the knot in 1285, and for a time, it

00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:01.420
looked as though a male heir was possible.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.080
Yolande was pregnant with their first child
when Alexander decided to ride from Edinburgh

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.850
to her side at Kinghorn Castle.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:11.810
He would never finish the journey, and a single
misstep by his horse changed history.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.870
It was dark, the weather was terrible, and
his horse slipped along the cliffside road

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:16.870
near Pettycur.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.010
Alexander fell, and his body was recovered
the following day.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.910
Yolande would ultimately lose their only child,
leaving the little Maid of Norway as the heir

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.110
to the Scottish throne.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.120
Six guardians were appointed to watch over
Margaret, who was just eight years old.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.070
Scotland turned to the English monarchy to
help secure her position as their new ruler,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.070
and England agreed.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:41.270
Their king, Edward I, drew up a treaty that
not only betrothed the little Margaret to

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:46.870
his own son — who would become Edward II
— but also guaranteed Scottish independence.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:52.260
In 1290, Margaret, the Maid of Norway, left
Bergen and headed to the aptly named St. Margaret's

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.310
Hope in South Ronaldsay.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:56.160
And there, she died.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.430
She passed away after suffering from severe
sea-sickness, and this is where the tale turns.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.930
Had Margaret wed Edward II, Scotland and England
would have been united — peacefully.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:11.780
Instead, Scotland was left with no immediate
ruler and now, England had a claim to stake.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:16.470
The Wars of Independence and a mysterious
hero

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:23.670
Now, historians pretty much agree that everything
we just talked about happened like we said.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.770
But when it comes to Wallace, things are a
little murky.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:31.320
He had already been born by the time Scotland
was plunged into the kind of chaos that only

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.640
comes when an entire nation is left without
a clear plan for succession, but there's no

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.910
consensus on even the basics, like when and
where he was born.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:45.470
The closest historians have been able to get
is somewhere around 1270, with both Elderslie

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:49.620
and Ellerslie laying claim to the honor of
being his birthplace.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.390
While Wallace definitely wasn't a nobleman,
he wasn't exactly a peasant, either.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:58.160
His father was a knight and a minor landowner,
and interestingly, he was likely from a family

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.430
of fairly recent immigrants to Scotland.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:04.630
His family name, Wallace, comes from "le Waleis",
which means "the Welshman".

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:08.340
It's thought that he was a descendent of a
Richard Wallace who had moved to Scotland

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.530
alongside the Stewarts only in the 12th century.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.210
What else do we know about his early years?

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.210
Not much.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.910
He was well educated, knew both French and
Latin, and that was likely thanks to tutelage

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.150
from his uncle, who was a priest at Dunipace.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.660
Given his later successes on the battlefield,
historians have surmised that he had military

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.510
experience before his rise as a revolutionary,
and it's even been suggested that he fought

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.570
for Edward I in various Welsh campaigns.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:38.050
It's also thought that Wallace had run-ins
with the law well before he answered the call

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.050
of the revolutionaries.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:40.050
It's even possible that he was known more
as an outlaw than any kind of upstanding knight.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.639
Wallace took the education that his uncle
had begun and headed to Dundee to finish it.

00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:47.540
According to one story, that's where he killed
the son of an English constable who had insulted

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:48.540
him.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.820
It's also believed that he killed several
English soldiers in Ayrshire after they confronted

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.980
him for illegal fishing, and it wasn't until
1297 that he became something more than a

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.280
common miscreant.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.570
And this is where we're going to take a moment
for a very important aside.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:05.040
There are only a few instances where a reliable
source recorded information about Wallace,

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.780
and most of those have to do with his two
most famous battles, Falkirk and Stirling

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.760
Bridge, and — spoiler alert — his execution.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.500
Aside from that, sources are a little iffy.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:21.690
In fact, the major source of information on
Wallace's life is a poet named Henry the Minstrel,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.300
who was better known as Blind Harry.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.630
He was born around 1440, and that means he
wrote his epic poem, The Wallace, a full century

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:32.720
and a half after the real Wallace's death.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.780
And here's where things get even more complicated.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:39.960
Blind Harry — who was, indeed, blind from
birth — claimed he wrote his epic based

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.960
on the writings of a John Blair.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.170
Blair, he said, was a Benedictine monk who
had been close friends with Wallace and who

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.590
had written a biography of him at the request
of a senior bishop.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:53.730
There's no actual proof this request was ever
made, though, even though it's technically

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:54.730
plausible.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:59.020
To go one step further, there's no proof that
a biography of Wallace ever existed, either,

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.050
but there is a fascinating story that makes
it possible that manuscript had actually been

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.050
written.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:09.389
An early version of Blind Harry's Wallace
poem included text by a monk named Arnold

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:10.900
Blair, not John Blair.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:15.190
Buried in another manuscript is a title for
that work, supposedly written about the Scottish

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:18.130
hero, along with a footnote that read, "Vide
Cottonian MSS."

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:21.130
And that's surprisingly important.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:26.170
The Cottonian Manuscripts were given to the
British Museum in 1700 by Sir John Cotton,

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:31.530
and only 31 years later, a fire destroyed
about a quarter of the manuscripts in the

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.530
collection.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.110
Was Wallace's biography among them?

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:38.350
We have no idea, and that means it's impossible
to trace many of the details of Wallace's

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.820
life back beyond Blind Harry.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:45.150
As if that isn't confusing enough, there are
plenty of details in Blind Harry's definitive

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.360
Wallace work that historians have proved to
be false.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.620
One of the very first assertions Blind Harry
makes is that William Wallace was the son

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:53.760
of Malcolm Wallace.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:54.760
He wasn't.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:59.150
In 1297, Wallace wrote a letter to the mayors
of the German towns of Lubeck and Hamburg.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:03.010
The letter has survived, along with Wallace's
own personal seal.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:07.850
It shows a hand drawing back the string and
arrow of a bow, and it includes a Latin inscription

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.240
that translates to "William, son of Alan Wallace".

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.330
That calls into question the historical accuracy
of one of the most famous and oft-repeated

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.300
episodes in Wallace's life.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.300
The popularly-told tale is that Wallace had
married a woman named Marion, and was visiting

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.300
the lady and their daughter when English soldiers
got wind of his location.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.960
He ran and rallied his own men, but Marion
was executed in the meantime and cemented

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.719
his determination to free Scotland from English
rule.

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:35.719
Did it happen?

00:08:35.719 --> 00:08:36.719
Maybe, maybe not.

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:44.930
The defiance of John Balliol and Stirling
Bridge

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:49.439
The English king didn't immediately step in
to take over rule of Scotland, and instead,

00:08:49.439 --> 00:08:54.870
he first tried to install a king who he was
fairly certain would obey him without question.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.680
That was John Balliol, and in all fairness,
it was the Scottish lords who asked Edward

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.550
I to choose a new ruler.

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:04.089
Balliol seemed to fit the bill in that he
was a descendant of former Scottish king David

00:09:04.089 --> 00:09:09.290
I, and the English crown also saw him as something
of a weak-willed successor who would remember

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:12.140
who put him in power — and be eternally
grateful for that.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:16.980
That was in 1290, and things went sideways
very quickly.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.740
England and France were embroiled in conflict,
and Balliol not only refused to support England,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:24.639
but in 1295 he formed an alliance with France.

00:09:24.639 --> 00:09:27.490
That, of course, didn't go over well at all.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:32.170
The following year, Edward marched into Scotland,
dethroned Balliol, and took over ruling Scotland

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.170
directly.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:37.559
Scottish nobles were imprisoned or found themselves
beholden to their English rulers, and countless

00:09:37.559 --> 00:09:42.250
Scottish soldiers were forced to march alongside
the English in their campaigns against the

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.250
French.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:47.449
Revolt came surprisingly fast, and even as
Balliol struggled to reestablish peace through

00:09:47.449 --> 00:09:51.019
more conventional means, the rebels believed
they had an advantage.

00:09:51.019 --> 00:09:54.680
The English army was fighting France, too,
and that meant they had fewer troops to send

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:56.050
against Scottish rebels.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:10:01.110
In 1297, those Scottish rebels kicked off
the First War of Independence at Lanark and

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:04.619
sadly, exactly what happened is unclear.

00:10:04.619 --> 00:10:08.800
Even Lanark's official history makes it clear
that no one really knows just why Wallace

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.649
chose to gather his supporters at that particular
location, but it is known that Wallace killed

00:10:13.649 --> 00:10:17.820
the occupying Sheriff of Lanark — along
with most of his men — and kicked off the

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:19.189
rebellion in full.

00:10:19.189 --> 00:10:23.000
This is, of course, where the story about
Wallace's wife and her execution comes into

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.410
the tale, but again, some historians believe
they've outright debunked the idea that she

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.660
even existed.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:34.480
Over the next months, Wallace and his rebels
hit targets in Dundee, Ancrum, and Scone.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.089
Other uprisings were led by Andrew Murray
and the MacDougall clan, and on September

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:44.699
11, 1297, things truly came to a head across
the River Forth at Stirling Bridge.

00:10:44.699 --> 00:10:49.070
The area presented some unique challenges
to advancing armies, the most famous of which

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:53.910
was very real — the bottleneck that restricted
the advancement of the English army.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.500
After a series of failed negotiations for
peace, the English army — led by the Earl

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.319
of Surrey — advanced across the river to
the northern side.

00:11:01.319 --> 00:11:05.550
Wallace was already camped there with his
troops and had the benefit of high ground;

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:09.100
he waited until a large portion of the English
calvary had crossed the bridge to charge with

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:13.740
his spearmen, pinning the calvary against
the river and slaughtering them wholesale.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:17.899
Only some of those that tried to flee actually
escaped the massacre, and it was a decisive

00:11:17.899 --> 00:11:22.179
battle that ended with the unraveling of the
Earl of Surrey's coalition army.

00:11:22.179 --> 00:11:25.940
Where some Scottish lords had marched alongside
him — including James Stewart, the High

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.649
Steward — that changed after Stirling.

00:11:28.649 --> 00:11:32.619
Stewart not only pulled his men out of the
English army, but he attacked the fleeing

00:11:32.619 --> 00:11:36.519
baggage train to encourage them to leave just
a little bit faster.

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:41.459
Among those the fleeing English army left
behind was Hugh de Cressingham, the Treasurer

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:42.459
of Scotland.

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:46.019
It's impossible to describe just how much
he was hated by the Scottish rebels, and his

00:11:46.019 --> 00:11:47.149
fate says it all.

00:11:47.149 --> 00:11:51.809
Cressingham was flayed, and some sources say
he was skinned while he was still alive, while

00:11:51.809 --> 00:11:53.379
others say he was dead.

00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:57.110
Pieces of his skin were distributed among
the victorious as trophies, and the legend

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:01.470
says further that Wallace took a particularly
large piece of skin and turned it into a belt

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.489
for his sword.

00:12:02.489 --> 00:12:06.899
The events of Stirling Bridge meant that Wallace
was a hero, sitting at the top of the victorious

00:12:06.899 --> 00:12:07.899
Scottish army.

00:12:07.899 --> 00:12:44.290
Unfortunately

00:12:44.290 --> 00:13:07.639
for Wallace, it

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:14.300
was a short-lived victory.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:29.389
The Guardian of Scotland

00:13:29.389 --> 00:13:40.449
After

00:13:40.449 --> 00:13:52.230
Wallace 
routed the English troops at Stirling Bridge,

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.230
he was honored at a ceremony in Kirk o' the
Forest.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:14:00.040
Scottish nobles named him Guardian of Scotland,
and this is one bit of the Wallace story that

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.680
historians know a lot about.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.829
In 2016, the Scottish Borders Council and
the University of Dunham conducted a geophysics

00:14:06.829 --> 00:14:11.769
survey that uncovered the ruins of the medieval
chapel where Wallace would have been honored

00:14:11.769 --> 00:14:13.050
hundreds of years ago.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:17.079
Unfortunately for the Scots, Wallace's triumph
would quickly turn sour.

00:14:17.079 --> 00:14:21.569
Edward I was a monarch not about to stand
for the humiliating defeat his troops had

00:14:21.569 --> 00:14:27.139
suffered at Stirling, and in 1298 he marched
on Scotland with thousands of calvary, infantry,

00:14:27.139 --> 00:14:30.999
and archers armed with one of England's greatest
weapons — the longbow.

00:14:30.999 --> 00:14:33.360
And that ended up being Wallace's weakness.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.930
Stirling Bridge may have been a triumph, but
this conflict at Falkirk was a brutal defeat

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:39.629
for Wallace and his rebels.

00:14:39.629 --> 00:14:43.439
The English monarch had learned from the mistakes
the military made at Stirling Bridge.

00:14:43.439 --> 00:14:47.769
Wallace had favored a technique where he put
his spearmen into a series of groups called

00:14:47.769 --> 00:14:52.199
schiltrons, and against calvary, it was a
brilliant and deadly strategy.

00:14:52.199 --> 00:14:57.189
Against longbows, it wasn't nearly as effective
and, for the first time, Edward I's longbows

00:14:57.189 --> 00:14:58.769
decimated an enemy.

00:14:58.769 --> 00:15:03.279
Those that didn't fall to the raining arrows
were sent fleeing by charges from the English

00:15:03.279 --> 00:15:07.899
calvary, and although both sides suffered
massive losses, Wallace lost something else

00:15:07.899 --> 00:15:09.170
— support.

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:13.220
Wallace retreated to regroup, and for the
next few years, he disappeared from mainstream

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:14.220
history.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.759
The hero who had, not long ago, sent letters
to the other leaders of Europe proclaiming

00:15:17.759 --> 00:15:23.170
Scotland's independence from England had escaped,
but he also resigned his position as Guardian

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.170
of Scotland.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:28.239
Over the next few years, he travelled abroad
and met with some of those same leaders, hoping

00:15:28.239 --> 00:15:30.529
to cement allies for the Scottish cause.

00:15:30.529 --> 00:15:34.779
Like the sources for much of Wallace's life,
sources that talk about his time abroad also

00:15:34.779 --> 00:15:39.239
vary greatly in the details, but there are
some fascinating tidbits to be gleaned from

00:15:39.239 --> 00:15:40.339
them.

00:15:40.339 --> 00:15:45.869
An outlaw and a traitor

00:15:45.869 --> 00:15:50.600
It's not 100 percent clear just where Wallace
went first, but it's likely his first stop

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:51.600
was Norway.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.459
From there, it seems as though he headed to
France, and a strange thing reportedly happened.

00:15:55.459 --> 00:16:00.069
Wallace's trip to France happened at the same
time Edward I was marrying his second wife,

00:16:00.069 --> 00:16:03.489
and that was Margaret, sister to France's
Philip IV.

00:16:03.489 --> 00:16:08.399
The marriage was, by most accounts, an unconditionally
happy one, and when the young queen was widowed

00:16:08.399 --> 00:16:12.459
at just 26 years old, she swore to never remarry
— and didn't.

00:16:12.459 --> 00:16:16.429
It also did what it was supposed to — it
brought the two long-time enemies together

00:16:16.429 --> 00:16:21.829
just long enough that England could turn her
sights to putting down rebellion in Scotland.

00:16:21.829 --> 00:16:25.899
And according to some sources, France's Phillip
IV was on such friendly terms with Edward

00:16:25.899 --> 00:16:30.070
I that when Wallace turned up at the French
court — no doubt expecting to find someone

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:34.369
who hated the English as much as he did — he
was actually arrested.

00:16:34.369 --> 00:16:38.149
Philip offered to hand Wallace over to the
English crown, but strangely, he was just

00:16:38.149 --> 00:16:41.249
thanked for the gesture and asked to keep
Wallace there.

00:16:41.249 --> 00:16:42.249
For safekeeping?

00:16:42.249 --> 00:16:46.350
Perhaps, but if there's anything history has
taught us it's that England and France just

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.670
can't seem to stay on the same side for long.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:53.249
Within the year, Wallace had gotten back into
the good graces of the French king.

00:16:53.249 --> 00:16:57.809
Not only was he released, but historians believe
they knew where he had planned on going next

00:16:57.809 --> 00:17:00.449
to plead his case for Scottish independence:
Rome.

00:17:00.449 --> 00:17:04.429
But here's where things get slightly complicated,
and it's also the perfect example of just

00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:08.130
how little we really know about Scotland's
largest hero.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.929
It's going to take a little bit of fast-forwarding,
all the way to 2011.

00:17:10.929 --> 00:17:15.300
At the time, there was a letter sitting in
the National Archives in London, and it was

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:20.220
said to be a safe conduct letter written by
Philip IV and addressed to the Pope.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:23.919
The letter confirmed Wallace's high standing
with the court of France, and asked the Pope

00:17:23.919 --> 00:17:27.839
to consider that when he was deciding whether
or not to hear Wallace's case.

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:31.840
There were two theories as to the provenance
of the letter.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:36.380
One suggested it had been taken from Wallace
when he was arrested by English forces in

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:42.510
1305, while another suggested it had been
carried by someone else entirely and intercepted

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:43.690
by spies.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:47.559
The letter was given to the National Records
of Scotland under a long-term loan agreement,

00:17:47.559 --> 00:17:49.779
but there's still a lot of mystery surrounding
it.

00:17:49.779 --> 00:17:53.340
There's no evidence to suggest Wallace ever
made it to Rome, and when it comes to the

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:57.419
question of whether or not he was carrying
the letter when he was arrested, the closest

00:17:57.419 --> 00:18:01.720
experts have been able to get to an answer
is "probably?".

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:02.720
And that's surprising.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:07.630
Wallace is this important, larger-than-life
figure that represents Scottish independence,

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:11.970
but when it comes to concrete facts and artifacts,
there's just not that much there.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.330
This letter — along with the letter to Lubeck
we mentioned earlier — is it, as far as

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:16.889
Wallace documents go.

00:18:16.889 --> 00:18:19.590
That said, we're getting to the end of Wallace's
tale.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.299
And, it's not a happy ending.

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:26.020
Some accounts suggest that around 1303, Edward
I offered Wallace the chance to surrender

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:30.060
and pledge his loyalty to the English crown
— several times, in fact.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.899
Wallace refused, and even that's up for historical
debate.

00:18:32.899 --> 00:18:37.820
The sources that claim clemency was offered
are English so… it's unclear just what the

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:38.820
truth is.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:42.690
It's not known exactly when he returned to
Scotland from traveling to Europe, but we

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:47.990
do know he was showing up in skirmishes across
the countryside in 1303.

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:52.610
After his refusal to bend the knee to England
and Edward I, Wallace was officially declared

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.470
a traitor to the crown and an outlaw.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:58.830
The declaration was a powerful one, and it
meant it was open season on him.

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:03.610
Anyone could kill him without mercy or feeling
the least bit guilty, and on the same note,

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.419
giving him aid or safe passage was considered
treason.

00:19:06.419 --> 00:19:10.480
It wasn't long before he was captured and
handed over to the English, by a fellow Scotsman

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.659
named Sir John Menteith.

00:19:12.659 --> 00:19:17.830
And this is where we can say, without a doubt,
that we definitely know exactly what happened

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:18.830
to Wallace.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:24.010
He was executed in London's Smithfield area
on Monday, the 23rd of August, 1305.

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:28.399
We know because the event — the spectacle,
even — was recorded by numerous monastic

00:19:28.399 --> 00:19:31.750
chroniclers, and for the most part, their
testimony was the same.

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:36.090
The execution itself was a long, gruesome
affair, but there was a method to the madness.

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:39.840
The punishments were handed out to fit the
crimes he had been found guilty of, and first

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.140
was the crime of treason.

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:45.559
As punishment, horses dragged him through
the streets of London to the site of his execution.

00:19:45.559 --> 00:19:49.799
Next was the punishment for murder and robbery,
and that was to be hanged until not quite

00:19:49.799 --> 00:19:50.799
dead.

00:19:50.799 --> 00:19:55.809
He was cut down, and then suffered his punishment
for the desecration of churches: he was castrated

00:19:55.809 --> 00:19:58.809
and disemboweled, his organs set on fire in
front of him.

00:19:58.809 --> 00:20:02.360
Whether or not he was aware of what was going
on, sources are vague — they do seem to

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:06.870
agree that when he was cut down from the gallows,
he was still very much alive.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:10.340
Lastly was the punishment for being named
an outlaw: beheading.

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:14.080
His head was mounted on London Bridge, and
what was left of the rest of his body was

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:20.480
cut into four parts and displayed in Newcastle-upon-Tyne,
Berwick-upon-Tweed, Perth, and Stirling.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.509
When we call the entire thing a spectacle,
it really was.

00:20:23.509 --> 00:20:28.250
His execution marked the opening of the Bartholomew
Fair, which was one of the largest communal

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.540
gatherings in 14th century England.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:34.320
There's a bit of irony here, too, in that
Saint Bartholomew was both the patron saint

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:36.130
of butchers and of tanners.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:40.409
Was Edward I making a statement, bringing
an end to Wallace's rebellion on the day dedicated

00:20:40.409 --> 00:20:42.460
to the patron saint of butchers?

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:45.940
It is, of course, impossible to say for sure,
but… some food for thought.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:50.220
Saint Bartholomew's apocryphal literature
has him learning the secret cosmic knowledge

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:55.649
from the divine, bringing together goats,
leopards, and even a werewolf in peace.

00:20:55.649 --> 00:21:00.360
Wallace reportedly skinned Hugh de Cressingham
and wore his hide as a belt long before he

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:04.840
was beheaded by the English king, and Saint
Bartholomew was both skinned and beheaded

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:09.039
before being dismembered and having his remains
scattered to the four winds.

00:21:09.039 --> 00:21:13.669
Much like Bartholomew became one of Christendom's
most tormented martyrs, Wallace became an

00:21:13.669 --> 00:21:19.380
even more important figure for Scottish resistance
after his execution, and remained so for hundreds

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:28.830
of years.

